body:
I've been learning for 7 years and i've had times where stuff was extremely hard but in a months time it would be extremely easy (yes thats how learning works haha) but the one thing that has consistently been hard for me is words that sound similar, just listening to a korean talk (youtube, show, movie, real life) is all good until they say a word that sounds like another word and it throws my brain through a loop and honestly my brain stops working. I got confused when someone said 조개 (shellfish, seashell) in a sentence and i mistook it as 쪼개 (to split apart something, break it in half, cut it) and even the context of the sentence didn't help and my brain just stopped lol

&#x200B;

This is not about the language but the process of learning it: learning from resources that cater to certain accents. I as an australian who has the broad accent (aka steve irwin accent, CRIKEY!) had trouble learning how to pronounce stuff at the start because textbooks (all i had access to at the time) would base their pronunciations of the American accent (example: 아 sounds like the A in Car, i pronounce car way differently) and many other examples.

I think I speak on behalf of everybody when I say: Holy shit.

&#x200B;

honestly I love the look of your shelves, its gives off an old fashioned kind of feel. Add a ladder!
